      "text": "To find the matrix $\\mathbf{P}$ for projecting onto the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, we need to determine the projection matrix for this direction. The shortcut formula for this is given by \n\nThe Going Linear Algebra way will yield\n\nSo the solution for problem above is our linear algebra answer. We can obtain the actual matrix starting from the determinant of $\\mathbf{P}$.\n\nAs you know the determinant of a special matrix is given by something like \n$$\\det( \\mathbf{P}) = \\pm k.$$  \nAs $\\mathbf{P}$ is an upper triangular matrix, we have $$\\det( \\mathbf{P}) = \\prod_{i=1}^n (\\lambda_i-0) = k.$$  \nBeing upper triangular, the determinant, the product of diagonal entries becomes the product of eigenvalues, be that positive, or negative.  \nSo here, is possible the eigenvalue is $-3$.  \nIf this number exist, and when the determinant is negative, its time to consider a formula or projection formula, which will give a complex number ( whether that represent multiply and divide into their roots, and then would need to do the roots of unity kind of problems).  \nSo for this by going the conventional course:\n$$\\begin{aligned} \\mathbf{P} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} + x\\begin{pmatrix} -3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\ & = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 \\end{pmatrix} + x\\begin{pmatrix} 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \\\\ & = \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 3x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 - 2x \\end{pmatrix} \\end{aligned}$$  \nThen as the determinant is $k = \\mathbf{P} ^{n-1} = 3 + 2x.  \n\\begin{aligned} 3 - 2x & = 0 \\\\ 2x & = 3 \\\\ x & = \\frac{3}{2} \\end{aligned}  \n\\begin{aligned} \\mathbf{P} &= \\begin{pmatrix} 1 + 3\\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right) & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 - 2\\left( \\frac{3}{2} \\right) \\end{pmatrix} \\\\ \\mathbf{P} &= \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{11}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\end{aligned}  \nTherefore, so that the inverse exists,  \n$$\\begin{pmatrix} 11 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 \\end{pmatrix} \