      "text": "To find the matrix for projecting onto the vector $\\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}$, we first normalize the vector. This can be done by dividing each element by the magnitude of the vector.\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{magnitude} &= \\sqrt{(-3)^2 + (-2)^2}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{9 + 4}\\\\\n&= \\sqrt{13}\\\\\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the normalized vector is $\\begin{pmatrix} -3/sqrt(13) \\\\ -2/sqrt(13) \\end{pmatrix}$. The projection matrix onto this vector is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\begin{pmatrix}\n-3/\\sqrt{13} & -2/\\sqrt{13} \\\\\n-2/\\sqrt{13} & -3/\\sqrt{13}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\n\nThen it is easier to find its inverse using the following equation:\n\nLet $A$ be the projection matrix. $A^{-1} = A^{T}(\\sqrt{det(A)})$\n\nTherefore, $A^{T}=\\begin{pmatrix}\n-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\n\\end{pmatrix}$ Since $\\sqrt{det(A)}=(\\pm 1)$\n\n$A^{T}(\\pm 1)=\\begin{pmatrix}\n-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} \\\\\n-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{13}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{13}}\n\\end{pmatrix}$ Therefore,\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA^{-1} &= \\frac{1}{(\\pm 1)}\n\\end{pmatrix}\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the inverse of the projection matrix does not exist.",
