      "text": "To solve for \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\), we start by identifying the vector onto which we are projecting. Let's denote this vector by \\(\\mathbf{u} = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix}\\).\n\nFirst, we calculate the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) that projects any vector onto the vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\). The formula for the projection matrix for a vector \\(\\mathbf{u}\\) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{\\mathbf{u} \\mathbf{u}^T}{\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u}}\n\\]\n\nWe first compute \\(\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u} \\mathbf{u}^T = \\begin{pmatrix} -3 \\\\ -2 \\end{pmatrix} \\begin{pmatrix} -3 & -2 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{u}^T \\mathbf{u} = (-3)^2 + (-2)^2 = 9 + 4 = 13\n\\]\n\nThus, the projection matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{P} = \\frac{1}{13} \\begin{pmatrix} 9 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 4 \\end{pmatrix} = \\begin{pmatrix} \\frac{9}{13} & \\frac{6}{13} \\\\ \\frac{6}{13} & \\frac{4}{13} \\end{pmatrix}\n\\]\n\nNext, we need to determine if \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) has an inverse. A matrix has an inverse if and only if it is both invertible and its determinant is non-zero. The determinant of the matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\left( \\frac{9}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{4}{13} \\right) - \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right) \\left( \\frac{6}{13} \\right)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\det(\\mathbf{P}) = \\frac{36}{169} - \\frac{36}{169} = 0\n\\]\n\nSince the determinant of \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is zero, \\(\\mathbf{P}\\) is a singular matrix and does not have an inverse. Therefore, the inverse matrix \\(\\mathbf{P}^{-1}\\) does not exist.\n\nHence, we enter the zero matrix to represent the result:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{\\begin{pmatrix} 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 \\end{pmatrix}}\n\\]",
